description:
# Accepted ltkj.2 implementation contract — CAP-FB-20260905-EMSCRIPTEN-ADMISSION-01

Authority: coordinator191 (2026-09-07) endorsed a Settings-only validation action writing the EXISTING registry/WAL without granting, registering or executing anything; a source-pinned numeric positive in an explicitly rebuilt acceptance copy using the REAL generated inventory and SW broker, default/release schema2 candidates/assets empty; numeric-only full admission and real image/link structural audits with explicit runtime/resource refusal pending later stages. Coordinator193 confirmed proceed after corrected independent different-model contract review (merger/Gemini, final12fce7c2). This description includes the complete implementable contract, not a pointer to private design notes. .1 is merged245cfdbb; latest checked origin/main9a090acd adds only release-copy/Beads correction. .3 starts only after .2 actually lands.

Readback corrections/pins: WasmPackageAuthority ALREADY reaches the Store SW through tool-exec-preview.js; the source test forbids a direct import spelling, not transitive presence. Do NOT move writes/decoding into Options/offscreen based on the withdrawn first-import claim (yk8w). Follow the real Settings/SW broker plan below; protect the unchanged 3000000-byte budget with actual production measurements. Standing5b budget2983291 leaves16709 bytes, but that is NOT a prediction of the new decoder delta. No dependency duplication, budget bypass or .3 host stub. Integer rules govern structural counts/indices/lengths, not signed finite f64 operation bounds. The numeric fixture domain [-1000000,1000000] is explicitly reviewed operation semantics, not a content-byte cap.

Implementation must distinguish syntax/typed structural validity, reviewed provider binding, runtime/profile eligibility, and inventory trust. Repinned semantic negatives must reach the intended guard; old-inventory tamper negatives only establish integrity. When testing graph/operation epoch coverage, compare identity digest independently of the version field. Source byte pins/inventory are the trust anchor; no new approval flag, callback, or parallel registry. Persist original failed runs/evidence and partial source on tooling failure; same-native supervisor recovery only. Parent owns Beads, review, commits and publication; initial worker authority is implementation/focused tests only, no commit/push/full suite/Chrome until separately scheduled.

### Measured A0 constraints

- Numeric: Wasm 278 bytes, SHA-256 `fea83472b7e56292785f132d0c5f564048cf34ae047627334f504b28dd405503`; glue 6155 bytes, `921175ec0ad89d8af17dbdffc49e58033c8717a246fe80b458f32e4a5a8584a9`. Zero native imports; defined memory min=max 256 pages (16 MiB), table funcref min=max 1, one global, no tags/custom sections. Native `cap_weighted_sum` is `(f64,f64,f64)->f64` from real C. Full exported types and global initializer still need extraction/pinning by the implementation's decoder, not inference from the A0 report.
- Image: Wasm 102166 bytes, `2a3fd8992dec90f0308d6d8e1776c13f6fa991a38a571542db85c929e1ddb817`; fixed 256-page memory, funcref table 216/216. Native imports `env.__assert_fail (i32,i32,i32,i32)->()` and `env.emscripten_resize_heap (i32)->i32`. Real emitted implementations in `build-a/image-resize.mjs` are `___assert_fail` (decode memory strings, abort) and `_emscripten_resize_heap` (abort OOM). Neither is a return-zero compatibility stub.
- Link: main `03f92aec27b6f91a0a5aec0ed5d3a2e4ab6fa83517b79413eacaa9859654ae3f`, 6139 bytes; side `2fa7665a097af7bb3d456e2846f9f0edd5c7f110e924c57b4befe2a65e2dffee`, 95 bytes. Main imports `env.side_increment (i32)->i32` from the side and resize-heap from glue. Main memory 256/256, table 1/**no encoded maximum**; side has no memory/table/imports. Main dylink.0 needed list is `["link-side.wasm"]`; both static allocation sizes are zero, main memory alignment exponent 2. No-eval linking worked in A0, but its unbounded table has no enforceable product allocation policy yet.
- All use Emscripten 6.0.0, manager commit `d223ae73c6998296e3ab27cf81dc2c2c9fd383de`, Emscripten commit `afa15e0c56d1292e073c2c91bafc1d5e0cdf0dd3`, worker ES module factory, no dynamic JS, no FS, fixed memory. Repeated build-a/build-b share installed SDK/cache, not hermetic reprovisioning.
- Native ambient bridge and side EM_JS/EM_ASM fixtures stay adversarial. Unsafe control reached storage/Worker; inspector-blocked egress is harness safety, not JS confinement. A0 positive imports are env/none, not schema-1 WASI.


### Intent and non-goals

Implement a closed bundled-only `schemaVersion:2`, `runtime.kind:"emscripten-module-v1"` branch in the existing WasmPackageAuthority. Validate exact executable assets, types, providers, operation and resource policy **before any package JS import, Wasm instance, constructor or tool invocation**. Record successful validation through existing registry/CAS/WAL and expose real Settings/broker validation, without giving packages execution or grant authority.

Preserve schema 1, `BUNDLED_ALLOWED_IMPORT_MODULES=["wasi_snapshot_preview1"]`, tiny/default/large memory rules, owner-uploaded-Wasm isolation, existing catalogue behavior, signature truth and removed arbitrary content-byte ceilings. No general JS sandbox claim, runtime loader, offscreen execution host, dynamic linker, pthread group, output custody, new authority registry, owner-supplied providers/hooks/URLs or no-op imports. Host `.3`, custody `.4`, linking `.5`, threads `.6` remain separate stages. Default developer/store builds ship zero Emscripten package/candidate assets and zero executable Emscripten catalogue rows until the later host/custody admission gate.

### Normative schema proposal

All objects below are **closed** (only listed keys), all listed fields required unless `?` appears; null is allowed only when stated. No arbitrary JSON extension object. All IDs use existing `ID_RE`, hashes lowercase 64-hex SHA-256, counts/indices/lengths use nonnegative safe integers, byte lengths positive for executable assets. Reuse existing package/version, signer, source, build, SBOM, license, metadata, capabilities and replay validators unchanged. UTF-8 JSON must pass the existing duplicate-key pre-parser and equal `canonicalJson`; do not hash unvalidated JSON. Keep depth/key safety bounds, not new content-byte caps. Arrays preserve order in hashing; unordered authority sets must have their specified sorted unique order, never silently normalize malicious input.

Top-level exact keys:

```
{
 schemaVersion: 2,
 package, signer, source, build, sbom, license, meta,
 tools: Tool[], runtime: Runtime, assets: Asset[],
 entry: Entry, modules: Module[], linkGraph: LinkGraph,
 resources: Resources, provenance: Provenance
}
```

`package/signer/source/build/sbom/license/meta` have **exactly schema-1 shapes and constraints**. `executables` is not allowed in schema 2; do not invent a main-Wasm field masquerading as whole-graph identity. `tools` retains exact schema-1 fields `{toolId,digest,capabilityDigest,replayClass,capabilities}`. Each tool maps bijectively to an entry operation; its digest is the operation digest defined below, and capabilities/replay exactly equal the operation's declaration. No aliases or tools without operations.

```
Asset = {id, role, path, sha256, size}
role = "adapter" | "glue" | "main-wasm" | "side-wasm" |
       "pthread-bootstrap" | "data"
Runtime = {
 kind: "emscripten-module-v1",
 abi: "emscripten-6.0.0-thin-native-v1",
 compiler: {version:"6.0.0", emsdkCommit, emscriptenCommit},
 glue: {format:"es-module-factory", environment:"worker",
        dynamicExecution:false, filesystem:false},
 features: Feature[], profileDigest
}
Feature = "mutable-globals" | "sign-extension" | "nontrapping-fptoint" |
          "bulk-memory" | "reference-types" | "simd128" | "exception-tags"
Entry = {adapterId, adapterAsset, glueAsset, mainAsset, operations: Operation[]}
Operation = {
 id, toolId, kind:"native-scalar-v1", exportName,
 params: ScalarParameter[], result: ScalarType,
 capabilities, replayClass, io: {kind:"none"}
}
ScalarParameter = {name, type:ScalarType, minimum, maximum}
ScalarType = "i32" | "f64"
```

Asset paths are existing strict package-relative path syntax, canonical ASCII, no `%`, `?`, `#`, backslash, empty/dot segments, schemes or traversal. Identity is `(id,role,path,sha256,size)` with IDs and paths unique, assets sorted by id. Wasm paths remain `extension/wasm/cas/<sha256>.wasm`; JS stays package-local under `extension/wasm/runtime/<package-name>/<version>/`, unchanged emitted bytes with `.mjs` suffix. Data uses an inventoried package-local path. `adapterAsset/glueAsset/mainAsset` must resolve to those exact roles, exactly one of each; every side/bootstrap/data asset must be accounted for by graph/policy, no spare executable asset. Schema describes pthread-bootstrap but initial policy refuses it explicitly before code; this is representation, not a hidden threading approval.

Runtime commits are 40-hex, pinned to the measured A0 commits above, not arbitrary same-version compilers. Feature list sorted unique. Require only features actually decoded (including type, section, initializer, element/data and instruction semantics), not a caller feature wish-list or custom `target_features` assertion. The accepted numeric feature set must be generated from its exact bytes and then pinned with independent decoder assertions. Unknown/non-enumerated features fail `runtime_feature_unsupported`. `profileDigest = SHA256(canonicalJson(runtime without profileDigest))`; its meaning is a compiler/feature contract, not a signature.

Operations sorted by id, IDs/tool IDs unique. Export name is bounded ASCII using the existing call-export name grammar; parameter names unique in order. Parameters have finite numeric min/max, min<=max; i32 boundaries are signed 32-bit integers. Native export signature must exactly equal ordered parameter/result types. Native-scalar-v1 has one result, no file/handle/string values, no optional args, no variadics, no caller export names. Reject non-finite input/output at the future adapter boundary. In this stage the only fully admissible operation profile is **`cap-a0-numeric-v1`**, with `cap_weighted_sum`, ordered f64 parameters `value,weight,bias`, bounds [-1000000,1000000], f64 result, capabilities `["compute"]`, replay `read-only`, io `none`. These proposed finite bounds avoid arithmetic overflow for this operation; they are operation semantics, not file-byte limits. No public tool registration results from the declaration.

```
Module = {
 asset, imports: Import[], exports: Export[],
 memories: Definition[], tables: Definition[],
 globals: GlobalDefinition[], tags: Definition[],
 start: null | Index,
 dylink: null | Dylink
}
Import = {module, symbol, kind, index, type, provider: Provider}
Export = {name, kind, index, type}
kind = "function" | "memory" | "table" | "global" | "tag"
Provider = {kind:"glue", asset, binding}
         | {kind:"module", asset, exportName}
Definition = {index, type}
GlobalDefinition = {index, type, initializer: ConstantExpression}
FunctionType = {params: ValueType[], results: ValueType[]}
MemoryType = {address:"i32", shared:boolean, min:Index, max:Index|null}
TableType = {element:"funcref", min:Index, max:Index|null}
GlobalType = {value:ValueType, mutable:boolean}
TagType = {attribute:0, signature:FunctionType}
ValueType = "i32" | "i64" | "f32" | "f64" | "v128" | "funcref"
ConstantExpression = {op:"i32.const", value:signed-i32}
                   | {op:"i64.const", value:canonical-signed-decimal-string}
                   | {op:"f32.const", bits:8-lowercase-hex}
                   | {op:"f64.const", bits:16-lowercase-hex}
                   | {op:"global.get", index}
                   | {op:"ref.null", type:"funcref"}
                   | {op:"ref.func", index}
Dylink = {
 memorySize, memoryAlignment, tableSize, tableAlignment,
 needed: RelativeName[]
}
LinkGraph = {
 policy:"none"|"eager", main:AssetId,
 dependencies: {from:AssetId, name:RelativeName, to:AssetId}[]
}
```

`type` is the kind-specific closed type above (no redundant unchecked typeIndex). Resolve every function/tag type index through the binary type section, including imported and defined function index spaces, before comparing structural signatures. Record resolved types for **every export**, not just called exports. Import indices are their kind's full index-space position; imports retain binary order. Export records retain binary export order; names unique. Modules sorted by asset ID. Definitions list **defined** objects only, indexed after imports in the appropriate kind space, in binary definition order. The combined imported/defined spaces are complete, including nonexported globals and tags. Reject invalid indices, mutability bytes, unknown kinds/value types and missing/invented records.

`Provider.glue.binding` is an exact reviewed local binding name in that asset's emitted import-object mapping; it is metadata, NEVER a reflective runtime lookup or callback supplied to admission. `Provider.module` must resolve an export of an admitted module of the same graph with compatible kind/type; no cross-package lookup. Ownership of imported memory/table/global/tag follows this provider to its single defining module; detect provider cycles and aliases to outside resources. Module-owned objects need no separate grant registry. Generic host/namespace/GOT providers are not in the initial union: a graph needing synthesized linker globals fails `provider_unsupported` until `.5` supplies real reviewed implementations. Do not label env as an authority.

`dylink` is null when absent. For present `dylink.0`, require exactly one MEM_INFO subsection; NEEDED may be absent (= empty array), otherwise exactly one. Preserve raw needed order/duplicates. Dependency edges are unique and sorted by `(from,name)`; each distinct needed name resolves exactly once to a side asset; graph contains exactly the reachable closure, with one main, no dangling/orphan/ambiguous/cyclic dependencies. Side modules may legitimately have zero memory. Names obey strict relative path syntax and are lookup keys, never filesystem or URL code sources. Reject unknown/duplicate dylink.0 sections/subsections, including unsupported export/import-info/TLS/embedded-JS semantics, rather than skipping them. Supporting them later is a separate ABI-profile expansion.

Alignment is a power-of-two **exponent**, sizes/alignments are u32; check alignment shifts and aligned sums using BigInt before safe-number conversion, reject overflow beyond memory32/table address space or resource budget. Traverse the fixed dependency order deterministically for accounting; preserve raw needed lists so future linking cannot quietly reorder constructor semantics. No runtime dlopen or side selection by input. `policy:none` requires only main and dylink null. `policy:eager` gets a real structural closure/type/provider audit, but is **not admitted to execution**, and initial whole-package admission rejects it with `link_runtime_unsupported` after structural auditing. This is not a pretend accept-all linker: valid A0 main+side graph must pass the structural audit, malformed variants must fail specific structural checks, and the final resource/runtime refusal is observable separately.

```
Resources = {
 class:"em32-unshared-fixed-16",
 memory:{owner:AssetId,index:Index,initialPages:256,maxPages:256,growth:false},
 table:{owner:AssetId,index:Index,initialElements:Index,maxElements:Index,growth:false},
 threads:{mode:"none"},
 io:{filesystem:"none",network:false,clock:false,random:false},
 lifecycle:{freshInstance:true,startupMs:10000,callMs:30000,concurrentJobs:1}
}
Provenance = {
 record:{path,sha256,size},
 comparison:"same-sdk-byte-identical", hermeticReprovisioned:false
}
```

Initial full admission: numeric fixed memory/table only; table must be exactly 1/1. Other bounded tables are representable/auditable but not automatically granted this measured profile. All modules' imported-memory types must be memory32, with compatible sharing and bounds; initial package has exactly one defined unshared memory. Memory32 min<=max<=65536 pages; no maximum is represented as null, never confused with 0. For a supplied memory/table, provider minimum must meet import minimum, and provider maximum must not exceed an import maximum when present; missing provider max cannot satisfy bounded import. Global types/mutability exact; tag attribute 0 and empty results, exact parameter signature; functions structural equality. Initial full admission refuses shared memory, memory64, multiple memories, unknown flags, unbounded memory/table, external/synthesized ownership and all tags. Typed parsing still reports valid tag metadata and validates section order before this explicit profile refusal.

The provenance sidecar is canonical duplicate-safe JSON, with a **closed** schema: `{format:"cap-emscripten-admission-provenance-v1",toolchain:Pin[],sources:Pin[],dependencies:Dependency[],patches:Pin[],buildScript:Pin,argv:string[],environment:{SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH:decimal-string},outputAssets:AssetId[],comparison:{kind:"same-sdk-byte-identical",assets:AssetId[],buildA:Pin,buildB:Pin,hermeticReprovisioned:false}}`; `Pin={name,source,revision,sha256,size}`, source is either strict relative source path or credential-free HTTPS provenance URL, revision nonempty bounded ASCII; `Dependency={name,version,source:Pin,license:Pin}`. Arrays of pins sorted by name, unique names; argv order retained, values bounded ASCII data, never executed by validator. This includes compiler/SDK/LLVM/Binaryen/archive, wrapper and build script pins; all output IDs exactly cover assets. Record `build.reproducible:false` conservatively; sidecar states narrower repeated-build evidence instead of claiming hermetic reproducibility. Source references need not all ship, but sidecar/SBOM/notices do and are hash-bound inventory files. Do not silently broaden license grammar or relabel dependencies.

### Binding reviewed implementation before host .3 exists

There is no .3 adapter implementation today. Add only a **small real capability-free numeric operation adapter asset** (native export invocation and exact scalar argument/result checks), with no glue import, factory creation, Worker, file, network or browser dependency. Its reviewed source and exact emitted bytes are inventoried. This is the operation implementation, not an execution host. Admission fetches/hashes it as data and never imports it. Do not copy the probe worker/unsafe registry or create fake factory callbacks.

The trust anchor is the existing generated inventory's **exact approved manifest digest**, which binds adapter bytes, glue bytes, operation contract, profile, and provider map. Generator reads source-reviewed fixture metadata and checks retained A0 hashes; it must not infer provider approval from whatever names the binary asks for. Numeric's map is genuinely empty because its native binary has zero imports and emitted `wasmImports={}`. Nothing is fabricated to satisfy a nonempty import list.

For graph-audit positive coverage, bind image's two imports to the actual emitted glue bindings described above, and link-main's side_increment to the actual side export. Those graph audits are not a promise that their operation adapters/resources are admitted. Unknown glue bindings, mutated glue bytes with old inventory, invented namespace providers, unresolved exports and type mismatches fail. Source review establishes binding-to-function meaning; Wasm types or a JavaScript string scan do not prove host-function safety. Until a corresponding reviewed execution adapter/profile exists, such a candidate cannot enter full admission. Never claim admission itself executed or confined JavaScript.

### Binary audit implementation requirements

Create a narrow reusable `extension/lib/emscripten-module-audit.js` consumed by the schema-2 authority, build and archive scans. Reuse existing error codes/style and hashing/canonical helpers; leave schema-1 parser/exports intact. It must:

1. Parse exact header, framing, bounded payload readers and standard section order **1,2,3,4,5,13,6,7,8,9,12,10,11** with custom sections interspersed; one of each standard section, reject unknown standard IDs. DataCount precedes code/data; validate its count against data segments and instruction requirements. Tag section is not numerically sorted after data.
2. Accept spec-legal bounded nonminimal LEB encodings in schema 2 (Emscripten linking can pad indices); check u32/i32/i64 byte counts, terminal unused/sign bits, overflow, truncation and section confinement. Do not apply schema-1's canonical-LEB policy by accident. JSON remains canonical.
3. Decode function types/index spaces, imports/definitions/exports, globals' constant expressions, tables/memory/tag types, start reference/signature, element/data modes and indices. Require function/code counts match. Reject unsupported recursive/subtype/GC types, externref, typed refs, memory64/table64, multi-memory, extended const or custom semantics explicitly.
4. Decode instruction **framing/features** for the supported MVP/scalar, selected reference/table/bulk-memory and SIMD forms needed by A0 (image may emit SIMD). Never byte-scan for opcodes inside immediates. An unsupported prefixed opcode fails explicitly. Delegate full stack/control/type validation to **`WebAssembly.validate(bytes)`**, never instantiate or compile a module with start side effects. Keep engine validation mandatory even after custom audit success. A0 numeric is the initial admission positive; image/link are audit coverage, not reason to add every proposal opcode.
5. Compare all decoded metadata with manifest declarations, check provider compatibility and closure, then resource/operation eligibility. Unknown features must fail even if the engine supports them. Start sections are represented/validated but initial admission rejects non-null start. Constructors exported as functions are recorded but never called.
6. Non-link A0 has no custom sections. Permit only recognized metadata custom sections with explicitly parsed format when actually needed; initially only dylink.0 in the audit profile. Do not skip arbitrary custom names, target_features claims or relocation/TLS/embedded-JS payloads.

### Digest, registry and inventory integration

- `operationDigest = SHA256(canonicalJson(Operation))`; require each matching tool.digest. All fields, bounds, capability/replay/I/O declarations participate.
- `graphDigest = SHA256(canonicalJson({runtime,assets,entry,modules,linkGraph,resources,provenance}))`. No embedded graphDigest field in its own input. Adapter/glue/module/bootstrap/data byte identity and operation contract all participate. Provenance sidecar hash participates.
- Existing manifest digest hashes full validated manifest without signer.sig; use signature scope `cap-wasm-manifest:v2\0<digest>`, while keeping `verified:false`/`not-implemented`. Manifest metadata/signature declaration is not independent publisher authentication.
- Schema-2 `_record.current` adds `runtime`, `graphDigest`, and `operationDigests`; compute existing `capabilityDigest` from `{runtime,graphDigest,operationDigests,tools:<existing identity projection>}`. Preserve schema-1 digest bytes exactly. Existing epoch `version:capabilityDigest` then rotates on graph OR operation drift without a second epoch store.
- Keep current.executables as **module-only stored audit summaries** `{id,sha256,size,...typed measured metadata}` for WAL compatibility; they are not a second manifest format and must not authorize JS from a main Wasm digest. Full validated manifest/graph identity accompanies them. Runtime-aware consumers fail closed rather than treating a schema-2 record as WASI.
- Same version changed manifest fails identity conflict; a reviewed newer inventory-bound version plus expected predecessor uses existing CAS/WAL update. Deduped validation still re-verifies assets. Revoked package/key remains rejected, offline recovery remains exact; retained grants/epochs from earlier graph fail. Failed verification must leave registry unchanged.
- `_verifyBundle` for schema 2 obtains **every** asset from the verified inventory by exact path and checks length/hash, plus sidecar/SBOM/notices, before binary audit. Do not trust caller files as providers or paths. Retain `admitBundled({manifest,files,expectedVersion})` compatibility for WASI; new broker reads packaged bytes only.
- Avoid stale `_inventoryFiles` cache across explicit validation requests: create a fresh real inventory/authority for validation and/or explicitly reload verified bytes per attempt. A successful check must not bless a later changed unpacked-tree asset because a previous check cached it.
- Extend generator/build/scan/archive mapping through one schema-aware manifest-to-assets helper, not three parsers. Reject undeclared physical Wasm/JS, duplicate path mappings, manifest digest drift, unknown asset roles and missing assets. Hash exact shipped JS, not pre-esbuild source only; simplest initial fixture is copied unchanged .mjs assets. No rewritten glue with stale hashes.

### Real Settings/broker proof without an execution host

Minimal proposed production seam (not present at clean245): a small `extension/lib/wasm-package-admission.js` broker service using the real inventory + WasmPackageAuthority, invoked by two exact Settings-only messages:

- `{type:"tool.package.validation-list"}` returns inventory-listed schema-2 package identities/version and validation availability only (no code/data content or selectable paths).
- `{type:"tool.package.validate",packageId,version,expectedVersion}` where expectedVersion is null or a valid version. Reject extra keys. Derive manifest path from the inventory identity, never message paths/bytes/profile/provider overrides. Require exact owner-options principal, exact options URL (existing allowed hash suffix), nonempty documentId, no page sender. Content scripts, hub, model tools, offscreen and arbitrary same-extension pages cannot invoke it.

Service calls real `admitBundled`, journals the existing registry and returns a closed success `{ok:true,packageId,version,manifestDigest,graphDigest,epoch,status:"validated-not-enabled",signatureVerified:false}` or failure `{ok:false,error,path}`. No grant issuance, tool registration or execution result. “Validate” does persist a package-validation record; UI must not call this a no-write operation. Ordinary tools still cannot invoke schema-2 records. No public API option such as `allowFixture`, `skipInventory` or `approved:true`.

Extend existing shared `<tool-library>` with one validation list/result property and explicit owner-button event wired in `options.js`, **separate from Run preview**. Preserve incumbent tokens/layout/components and current keyboard/live-region/error patterns. Copy: “Validate package”; success “Package validated. Execution is not enabled.” Empty release state “No Emscripten packages are available for validation in this build.” Do not say installed, trusted signature, runtime ready or native result. Update gallery from the same component source. This is a diagnostics extension, not a package installation/product catalogue redesign.

Safe fixture supply: extend the existing generator with an explicit **build-time-only acceptance target** in a durable source/build copy. Target chooses only tracked, source-reviewed numeric fixture metadata/pins and emits its real manifest/assets into the existing BUNDLED_INVENTORY, with no executable catalogue row. Ordinary generation has no schema-2 candidates. This target must not accept arbitrary asset roots/manifests from the browser. It is a test artifact construction input, not an owner-controlled approval mechanism. The acceptance copy builds/rebundles the actual SW inventory import; injecting files after building without regenerating/rebundling inventory is invalid proof. No patched globals, alternate validator, mocked registry, fake normalizer or runtime test oracle.

Run real Settings click → real runtime message broker → packaged fetch → typed audit → real OPFS-backed registry/WAL → rendered validation receipt. Observe no glue script evaluation, no Module/Instance creation, no root/pthread Worker and no native export call. Engine validation alone is permitted. For negative copies, retain the accepted generated inventory and change exactly one asset/manifest before Chrome starts; reload a fresh authority to defeat cache masking. Use separate disposable durable copies; never mutate the reviewed source or another lane's running tree.

A separate negative parser/type fixture may deliberately have updated test inventory hashes so it reaches semantic validation rather than failing the first digest check. Its record is still subject to the **same real** schema/provider/profile/operation rules. Label these constructed validator negatives, not authorized production candidates. Do not mutate the trusted provider declaration together with a malicious import to manufacture approval.

### Falsification and acceptance

1. Numeric fixture full admission succeeds via real authority and loaded Settings/broker. Its numeric operation is **not run** in `.2`; A0's observed 42.5 is historical ABI evidence, not this route's output. Repeat validation dedupes; durable query/restart sees same graph/epoch; revoke denies revalidation. Regular release/developer inventories/catalogues contain no Emscripten candidates or execute route.
2. Real A0 numeric/image/link binaries exercise full typed metadata. Numeric has no imports; image imports resolve to exact reviewed emitted functions; link import resolves to real typed side export. Image operation/host eligibility and link's unbounded table/link-runtime eligibility remain explicit refusal, not blanket graph-parser failure. Missing provider, wrong kind/signature/global mutability/tag signature/table element/memory owner or wrong dependency gives its own pre-execution refusal.
3. One-change tests for every schema field category and each nested unknown field; runtime/schema mismatch, unknown feature/role/profile, duplicate JSON at nested/escaped keys, noncanonical JSON, unsafe paths/percent/query/case substitution, duplicate IDs/paths/exports, orphan asset/module and missing dependency. Hash-only and **repinned semantic** negatives test different gates.
4. Tamper independently main Wasm, glue, adapter, side, data, provenance, operation parameters/result/capability/replay and inventory identity/length. All fail against retained inventory before import. A pthread-bootstrap asset is explicitly unsupported even with matching hashes; its corrupt/missing bytes never turn it into eligible code. No positive pthread claim is possible without `.6`.
5. Decoder cases: legal padded vs overflowing/truncated LEB; duplicate/order/unknown sections; correct/incorrect DataCount placement/count; tags in legal/illegal position and invalid attribute/type index; function/code mismatch; unknown kind, out-of-range type/export/start/element/data indices; invalid global initializer/mutability; invalid limits/sharing flags, memory64, max-less/multi-memory, table64, unsupported types/opcodes/custom subsections; dylink alignment/size overflow and duplicate/unresolved names. Engine-invalid bodies with superficially correct metadata must fail `WebAssembly.validate`; valid unsupported features must fail the profile, not silently pass.
6. Graph or operation contract change with reviewed newer inventory version changes current.capabilityDigest/epoch. Same-version rewrite fails, stale expectedVersion fails, revoke/update race has one winner, WAL fault points compensate/commit correctly, previous token never matches new graph. Use existing source-named registry tests; do not add a second store.
7. Loaded wrong-sender tests (page/content, hub, arbitrary extension document, missing documentId) and malformed message keys fail before fetch/write. Owner cannot supply `Module`, hooks, byte arrays, URL/export/provider/memory settings. Error displays are escaped/textContent; keyboard button and live result announcement work. Run preview and model catalogue remain unchanged.
8. Default generation and Store packaging prove schema-2 empty; unknown/unmanifested executable still fails physical scan/reachability. Exactly scoped engine-validate allowance must reject changed API to compile/instantiate/Instance, extra calls, wrong path/arg shape and broad scanner exemption. No-eval/CSP and owner-upload lane tests remain intact.
9. Independently reviewed tests must fail under mutants: omit asset loop → glue tamper goes green incorrectly; drop provider type comparison → repinned wrong signature accepted; omit operation digest → epoch unchanged; numeric ID section sort → valid data-count/tag fixture refused; remove engine validate → invalid body accepted; bypass inventory → changed candidate accepted; reuse cached inventory → second validation misses drift; leak catalogue row → public-empty guard red. Record actual RED/GREEN only during authorized implementation.

